+<section id="q2result">
+  <h2>What the parent test found</h2>
+  <p>The judge’s final record holds 11,028 lines on the 600 development poles <span class="src">spec §12</span>. On the poles’ own controls it was right 92.4% of the time, so the test is valid. On the 1,238 late-born clusters, against random decoys, it was right 81.2% of the time. That gives p<sub>u</sub> = 0.265, with a 95% interval of 0.211–0.321 <span class="src">metrics.json, run 7</span>. The lower end is above 0.20, so the rule labels the result packing.</p>
+  <p>Birth order was the second readout. Among the 574 poles with at least two jobs, the main job was born first in 47.6% (43.6–51.7%). Across 10,000 shuffles of the birth order within each pole, 97.5% give a share of 44.9% or less. The main job is the oldest more often than chance, p = 0.0005 <span class="src">spec §11</span>. The essay expects this of a modern architecture; the spec counts it as support for recursive generalization, not as the claim itself <span class="src">spec §1, §6.4</span>.</p>
