+  <h3>Checks on the answering agents</h3>
+  <p>After the API limit, Claude Code subagents of Sonnet 5.5 answered every judge line, in chunks of up to 15, two agents at a time. Each answer was audited against the agent’s own transcript before it entered the data. A checker found that this audit gated imports only from 10:29Z on 22 Oct <span class="src">journal 24 Oct 00:12Z</span>. Four groups of answers were then answered again by fresh agents under the hardened audit:</p>
+  <ul>
+    <li>375 judge lines from 25 chunks whose agents read their input through the shell or edited answers by script; 24 answers changed <span class="src">journal 24 Oct 01:05Z</span>.</li>
+    <li>Nine judge answers that belonged to a neighbouring request; two changed <span class="src">journal 24 Oct 00:20Z</span>.</li>
+    <li>205 clustering answers: 200 whose agents read excerpts of the contexts, and five whose reading could not be audited. This moved 27 clusters into or out of the late-born set, and 78 new judge lines covered the 13 that entered <span class="src">journal 24 Oct 02:17Z</span>.</li>
+    <li>One judge line whose input never reached its agent whole, found by checking every one of the 12,370 imported requests line by line against the transcript that answered it; same verdict <span class="src">journal 24 Oct 01:50Z</span>.</li>
+  </ul>
+  <p>There were seven unblind runs: runs 4, 5 and 6 followed these corrections, and runs 2, 3 and 7 added a readout or the texts to the sample files. Across them p<sub>u</sub> stayed between 0.260 and 0.265, and no label changed <span class="src">spec §11</span>.</p>
+
+  <h3>When do the writer’s texts start to fire?</h3>
+  <p>An extra GPU run on 23 Oct scored the writer’s texts for OLMo’s audit poles at all 16 checkpoints <span class="src">journal 23 Oct 23:32Z</span>. For each pole, the reading asks when its blind maximum first reaches 0.3 of its final score. The median is T<sub>3</sub>, about 13 billion training tokens. The prediction was a curve that rises in most poles and reaches the line earlier for one-job poles <span class="src">journal 20 Oct 02:58Z</span>. Only 1.0% of per-pole curves never fall by more than 0.05, although the median curve over poles rises at every checkpoint but one. One-job poles reached the line at T<sub>4</sub> and not-understood poles at T<sub>1</sub>; but a pole whose blind maximum is weak at T<sub>15</sub> reaches 0.3 of it early, and most such poles are not understood, so this comparison is biased. Also, 111 of 598 poles were already above the line at T<sub>0</sub>, the untrained model, so for them this reading cannot separate a job forming from the random-weights floor <span class="src">RESULTS.md footnotes</span>. This reading is exploratory; no rule uses it.</p>
