+<section id="problems">
+  <h2>Known problems</h2>
+  <p><strong>The negative poles’ vocabulary.</strong> The explainer’s prompt shows a pole’s most promoted and most suppressed output tokens. The harvest already stores each pole in its own sign, and the explain code swaps the negative pole’s two lists a second time. So every negative pole’s prompt listed the opposite pole’s tokens. This held for all 300 negative poles of each audit model and all 60 of each discovery round, and the second reader’s prompts carry the same swap <span class="src">explain requests against vocab.json</span>. The last-layer single-token suppressor flag is computed from the swapped lists too; it flags 0 poles under either view. The error was found on 21 Oct while building the study’s data explorer (infra/pg/neurons_explore.py), and it is not in the study’s journal.</p>
+  <p>The pass rates show no clear sign of it. Over the 300 neurons of each audit model, negative poles passed 70.0%, 64.0% and 59.7% in Qwen, Gemma and OLMo, against 67.3%, 64.0% and 62.0% for positive poles; rare and declined poles count as not passing. Among neurons where only one pole passed, the positive pole was the one 47, 53 and 63 times, and the negative pole 55, 53 and 56 times. The two poles of a neuron are different objects, so this comparison neither bounds nor measures the effect; no run explained a negative pole with its own list. The prompts’ 30 top contexts and activation statistics were not affected.</p>
